    "final_answer": "I have successfully completed both tasks using Wolfram Alpha. Here are the results:\n\n## Mass of Jupiter Compared to Earth\nAccording to Wolfram Alpha:\n- **Jupiter's mass**: 1.898 × 10²⁷ kg\n- **Earth's mass**: 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg\n- **Jupiter is 317.8 times more massive than Earth** (31,680% larger)\n\n## Length of One Day on Jupiter\nAccording to Wolfram Alpha (Jupiter's rotation period):\n- **Sidereal day**: 9.925 hours\n- Equivalent to **9 hours, 55 minutes, 30 seconds**\n- **35,730 seconds** (or 595.5 minutes)\n\n### Summary\nJupiter is approximately **318 times more massive** than Earth, and one day on Jupiter (its sidereal rotation period) lasts only about **9 hours and 56 minutes** — less than half the length of an Earth day. This rapid rotation also contributes to Jupiter's noticeable equatorial bulge.",
